To be effective, it would also have to have its centre of mass behind 
the point of impact. Arrows, spears and the like are designed to ensure 
that this is the case. Spinning a weapon on an axis perpendicular to its 
direction of travel works too, e.g. throwing axes, assuming the handle 
doesn't hit first.

Cutting with a sword in hand is harder than it looks: see the lengths 
gone to in practising Japanese sword techniques. Cutting with a sword 
not in hand is something I'd have to see to believe.


